_GUIDE_TOPICS.update({
"track-surface": """TRACK-SURFACE — TRACK-AWARE MULTISENSOR RECONSTRUCTION
Use when the magnetic amplitudes and dipoles are correct but the raster still shows parallel acquisition-lane texture because along-track sampling is much denser than cross-track spacing.

Recommended 0.50 m multisensor / 0.25 m raster start:
  mspy prm EXPORT -o Site_TRACK_SURFACE --site-name Site --phase-repair sensys-native --frame-heading smooth --heading-window 1.5 --grid-source points --point-method track-surface --track-cross-method adaptive --track-cross-tracks 4 --track-max-gap 1.25 --track-edge-extrapolation 0.25 --cell-size 0.25 --fill-distance 0 --cores 8

Processing:
  1. Preserve each real source+sensor trajectory.
  2. Regularize extreme along-line oversampling at a small interval (automatic 0.125 m for 0.25 m output).
  3. At every raster node evaluate independent neighbouring tracks, not dozens of samples from one lane.
  4. Adaptive mode uses a multi-track local linear fit in quiet field to reduce lane striping.
  5. Around strong anomalies it switches to bounded bracketing interpolation so compact dipoles are not blurred or overshot.
  6. Wider unsupported gaps remain NoData.

Audit rasters:
  independent_track_count, bracketing_status, cross_track_gap, along_track_support_misfit, nearest_track_distance and point_support_distance. These are support/QC layers, not magnetic data.

If real line-level offsets remain after track-surface, diagnose leveling separately; do not simply increase interpolation radius until the stripes disappear.
""",
})

_GUIDE_TOPICS.update({
"clean-map": """CLEAN MAP — STRONG COMPACT ARCHAEOLOGICAL CLEANING
Use when the Bz or reconstructed raster still has obvious acquisition tracks, directional striping, uneven regional background and fine high-frequency noise.

Recommended Rupea start:
  mspy clean-map Results/Rupea_THIN050_FORCE -o Rupea_CLEAN --source-product Bz_grid --profile strong --cores 8

The chain is deliberate: robust regional background flattening -> spectral stripe/track direction detection -> archaeology-protected directional suppression -> edge-preserving anisotropic/TV ensemble -> restrained final smoothing. The source Bz is never overwritten.

Profiles:
  balanced: broader 12 m background, lighter stripes/denoise, minimal smoothing;
  strong: default 8 m background, 0.70 directional suppression, strong edge-preserving denoise;
  very-strong: 5 m background and stronger suppression; use only as an interpretation/display branch.

Outputs are compact: clean_map, optional clean_smooth, one total removed-component raster, one comparison PNG and one JSON report. If coherent walls/ditches appear in the removed raster, reduce strength or use balanced.
""",
"regularize-points": """REGULARIZED POINT NETWORK — EXACT DERIVED 0.25 M LATTICE
This is different from physical thinning and different from ordinary raster interpolation. It creates exact new point coordinates (for example every 0.25 m in X and Y) and estimates each value from nearby real measurements.

Recommended Rupea command:
  mspy regularize-points Results/Rupea_THIN050_FORCE -o Rupea_REGULAR025 --spacing 0.25 --kernel-radius 0.35 --support-distance 0.55 --cores 8

Every measured point contributes to surrounding lattice nodes. A second robust pass down-weights local residual outliers. Normalized local weighting creates a uniform support network while --support-distance prevents large unsurveyed gaps from being fabricated.

The output ASC marks every row as RECONSTRUCTED_REGULAR_NETWORK. These are derived nodes, not physical sensor measurements. Keep the original ASC/Bz for quantitative provenance.

Useful follow-up:
  mspy clean-map Results/Rupea_REGULAR025 -o Rupea_REGULAR025_CLEAN --source-product regularized_0p25m --profile strong --cores 8
This combination is intended specifically for clean, visually uniform archaeological interpretation maps while keeping the measured source separate.
"""
})


_GUIDE_TOPICS.update({
"presentation": """PRESENTATION / INTERPRETATION DISPLAY
Purpose: create a publication/interpretation image with a quiet magnetic context and progressively emphasized coherent anomalies, without pretending the display is the measured field.

Recommended sequence:
  native/regularized raster -> deterministic cleaning -> presentation.

Example:
  mspy presentation Results/Site/CLEAN -o Site_PRESENTATION --source-product clean_map --mode publication --threshold-mode robust-z --threshold 1.3 --full-threshold 2.8 --context-smooth 0.45 --context-gain 0.32 --contrast-mode asinh

Modes:
  publication  keeps strong anomalies at full contrast and only faint background context;
  soft-fade    continuously suppresses weak texture;
  anomaly-only suppresses sub-threshold residuals;
  flat         gives the strongest neutral-background presentation.

Anomaly emphasis is blended continuously from a smoothed local magnetic context rather than pasted over a binary mask. Positive and negative anomalies are preserved by default. The source raster is never overwritten. Presentation products are DISPLAY/INTERPRETATION ONLY and should always be accompanied by the quantitative/native raster in scientific work.""",

"filtering": """MANUAL MAGNETIC FILTERING
The `filter` command exposes explicit, auditable filters instead of automatically applying a large processing suite.

Useful controls:
  --low-pass METRES             Gaussian low-pass sigma; suppresses short-wavelength noise but blurs small anomalies.
  --high-pass METRES            subtracts a broad Gaussian background; useful for regional/geological drift but can remove broad archaeology.
  --median-low-pass METRES      robust smoothing for spikes/ferrous speckle.
  --median-high-pass METRES     robust local-background subtraction.
  --fft-filter ...              Butterworth/Gaussian spectral low/high/band-pass.
  --upward-continuation METRES  potential-field smoothing that preferentially suppresses shallow/high-frequency sources.
  --remove-plane                remove a broad planar slope.
  --directional-notch           suppress long repetitive acquisition/plough stripes; always inspect the removed component.

Example:
  mspy filter Results/Site/CLEAN -o Site_FILTER --source-product clean_map --high-pass 6 --low-pass 0.20 --cores 8

The input is never overwritten. By default the command creates only the final filtered raster, total removed component, comparison image and report. Use --export-steps only when you need every intermediate stage.""",

"total-field": """TOTAL-FIELD / CAESIUM MAGNETOMETRY
MagSurveyPy includes a scalar total-field branch for Geometrics-style archaeological surveys. Preferred input is an exported DAT containing X Y READING TIME DATE LINE MARK. Raw STN is also supported when marker records are available.

Marks and lines
  Physical MARK records are important constraints because they segment traverses and help distinguish baseline changes from spatial anomalies. If a DAT has valid XY but no LINE/MARK labels, --synthetic-marks can infer labels from the existing point geometry. It never creates magnetic readings or moves points. Synthetic marks become useful when combined with --line-level mark.

Corrections
  --base-file BASE.dat       removes time-varying base-station field changes after interpolation to field timestamps.
  --igrf trend               removes only temporal IGRF model variation relative to the survey median.
  --igrf absolute            subtracts the modeled main field; requires ppigrf and site coordinates.
  --line-level mark          robust segment levelling constrained by marks.
  --deslope-grid             removes a robust plane per local grid; use carefully because very broad archaeology can also be removed.

Assembly
  Multiple local grids such as C1.dat ... C5.dat can be assembled automatically from natural grid names, or explicitly with --layout-csv. Local XY and measured field values are preserved in the corrected-point export.

Literature-inspired presets
  high-resolution-scalar emphasizes dense scalar acquisition/resampling and restrained image processing.
  deslope-highpass enables per-grid desloping and a transparent high-pass interpretation branch. These presets implement published principles, not an exact reproduction of unpublished author-specific processing recipes.

Example:
  mspy total-field GridFolder -o Foeni_TOTAL_FIELD --site-name Foeni --assemble auto --cell-size 0.25 --line-level mark --preset deslope-highpass --cores 8

Always retain *_total_field_corrected.tif as the corrected scalar reference. *_total_field_archaeology.tif is a derived archaeological branch.""",

"geometrics": """GEOMETRICS DAT / STN
DAT: expected fields are X Y READING and, when present, TIME DATE LINE MARK. Geometrics exported DAT is preferred because positions and mark-derived geometry are already explicit.
STN: type-3 marker records constrain positions and type-0 records contain sensor readings. MagSurveyPy reconstructs intermediate positions between physical markers without inventing bends.
For dual-sensor STN choose --sensor 1, --sensor 2 or --sensor mean. Use mean only when the sensors are scientifically appropriate to combine. If a proper DAT export contains both sensor trajectories as separate positioned readings, process the DAT directly."""
})


def _print_guide(topic=None):
    if not topic:
        print("Detailed MagSurveyPy guide topics:")
        print("  "+"  ".join(sorted(_GUIDE_TOPICS)))
        print("\nUse: mspy guide TOPIC")
        return 0
    t=str(topic).lower()
    if t not in _GUIDE_TOPICS:
        raise SystemExit(f"Unknown guide topic '{topic}'. Available: {', '.join(sorted(_GUIDE_TOPICS))}")
    print(_GUIDE_TOPICS[t].strip())
    return 0




# =============================================================================
# SUPPORTED-SURFACE INTERPOLATION + ADAPTIVE CLEANING
# =============================================================================

def _v12_robust_sigma(a):
    import numpy as np
    f=np.asarray(a,float)
    f=f[np.isfinite(f)]
    if not len(f):
        return 1.0
    med=float(np.median(f))
    mad=float(np.median(np.abs(f-med)))
    s=1.4826*mad
    return s if s>1e-12 else max(float(np.std(f)),1e-9)


def _v12_project_density_raster(source, original_input):
    """Locate the directly-observed measurement-density raster when available."""
    from pathlib import Path

    src=Path(source)
    inp=Path(original_input)

    candidates=[]

    if inp.is_dir():
        candidates += list((inp/"GeoTIFF").glob("*_measurement_density.tif"))
        candidates += list(inp.glob("*_measurement_density.tif"))

    candidates += list(src.parent.glob("*_measurement_density.tif"))

    prefix=src.stem
    if prefix.endswith("_Bz_grid"):
        prefix=prefix[:-8]
    exact=src.parent/f"{prefix}_measurement_density.tif"

    if exact.exists():
        return exact

    unique=sorted(set(p.resolve() for p in candidates))
    return unique[0] if len(unique)==1 else None


def _v12_surface_predict(method, grid, observed, targets, args):
    """Predict supported surface cells while preserving observed cells exactly.

    MagSurveyPy exposes both classical methods and archaeology-safe variants.
    High-order candidates are passed through the interpolation artifact guard by
    default; ``*-bounded`` forces local-envelope bounding even if the global
    guard was disabled. ``dipole-safe`` is a positive-weight local hybrid and
    cannot mathematically overshoot its neighbouring measured values.
    """
    import numpy as np
    from scipy.ndimage import distance_transform_edt
    from scipy.spatial import cKDTree
    from scipy.interpolate import griddata, RBFInterpolator
    method=str(method).lower().strip()
    force_bounded=method.endswith('-bounded')
    base_method=method[:-8] if force_bounded else method
    if base_method in {'local-idw','local-idw'}: base_method='idw'
    elif base_method in {'tin-linear','tin-linear','tin-linear'}: base_method='linear'
    elif base_method in {'tin-cubic','tin-cubic'}: base_method='cubic'
    elif base_method in {'survey-local','archaeology-safe'}: base_method='dipole-safe'
    if base_method=='bicubic':
        # Scattered/missing raster support has no strict tensor-product bicubic
        # definition. Clough-Tocher is the appropriate smooth piecewise-cubic
        # scattered-data analogue; the report records this explicitly.
        base_method='cubic'
    out=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,dtype=float); out[observed]=grid[observed]
    if not targets.any(): return out
    obs_rc=np.column_stack(np.nonzero(observed)).astype(float)
    vals=np.asarray(grid[observed],float)
    tar_rc=np.column_stack(np.nonzero(targets)).astype(float)
    tree=cKDTree(obs_rc)

    def _idw_prediction(power=None,neighbors=None):
        k=min(max(1,int(neighbors if neighbors is not None else args.idw_neighbors)),len(vals))
        dist,idx=tree.query(tar_rc,k=k,workers=max(1,int(args.cores)))
        if k==1: dist=dist[:,None]; idx=idx[:,None]
        exact=dist<=1e-12
        pp=max(float(power if power is not None else args.idw_power),1e-6)
        w=np.where(exact,0.0,1.0/np.maximum(dist,1e-12)**pp)
        nv=vals[idx]; pred=np.sum(w*nv,axis=1)/np.maximum(np.sum(w,axis=1),1e-30)
        rows=np.any(exact,axis=1)
        if rows.any():
            first=np.argmax(exact[rows],axis=1); pred[rows]=nv[rows,first]
        return pred

    if base_method=='nearest':
        _,idx=tree.query(tar_rc,k=1,workers=max(1,int(args.cores))); pred=vals[idx]
    elif base_method=='idw':
        pred=_idw_prediction()
    elif base_method=='dipole-safe':
        # Conservative local-linear surface where supported, positive-weight
        # IDW fallback elsewhere. Both are convex/local and therefore do not
        # generate spline-style overshoot around strong +/- anomalies.
        max_obs=max(10000,int(args.max_surface_observations))
        if len(obs_rc)>max_obs:
            step=int(np.ceil(len(obs_rc)/max_obs)); oo=obs_rc[::step]; vv=vals[::step]
        else: oo=obs_rc; vv=vals
        pred=np.asarray(griddata(oo,vv,tar_rc,method='linear'),float)
        fallback=_idw_prediction(power=max(1.8,float(args.idw_power)),neighbors=max(8,int(args.idw_neighbors)))
        bad=~np.isfinite(pred); pred[bad]=fallback[bad]
    elif base_method in {'linear','cubic'}:
        max_obs=max(10000,int(args.max_surface_observations))
        if len(obs_rc)>max_obs:
            step=int(np.ceil(len(obs_rc)/max_obs)); oo=obs_rc[::step]; vv=vals[::step]
        else: oo=obs_rc; vv=vals
        pred=np.asarray(griddata(oo,vv,tar_rc,method=base_method),float)
    elif base_method.startswith('rbf-'):
        kernels={'rbf-linear':'linear','rbf-cubic':'cubic','rbf-thin-plate':'thin_plate_spline','rbf-multiquadric':'multiquadric','rbf-inverse-multiquadric':'inverse_multiquadric'}
        if base_method not in kernels: raise SystemExit(f'Unknown RBF surface method: {method}')
        nbh=min(max(8,int(args.rbf_neighbors)),len(vals))
        kwargs=dict(kernel=kernels[base_method],smoothing=max(0.0,float(args.rbf_smoothing)),neighbors=nbh)
        if kernels[base_method] in {'multiquadric','inverse_multiquadric'}: kwargs['epsilon']=max(float(getattr(args,'rbf_epsilon',1.0)),1e-6)
        model=RBFInterpolator(obs_rc,vals[:,None],**kwargs)
        pred=np.empty(len(tar_rc),float); chunk=20000
        for i in range(0,len(tar_rc),chunk): pred[i:i+chunk]=model(tar_rc[i:i+chunk]).ravel()
    elif base_method=='ordinary-kriging':
        tmp=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,float); tmp[observed]=grid[observed]
        maxpx=max(1,int(np.ceil(float(args.surface_distance)/max(float(args.cell_size),1e-12))))
        kk=ordinary_kriging_limited(tmp,maxpx,float(args.cell_size),int(args.kriging_neighbors),float(args.kriging_range),float(args.kriging_nugget),int(args.cores))
        pred=kk[targets]
    elif base_method=='natural-neighbor':
        tmp=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,float); tmp[observed]=grid[observed]
        maxpx=max(1,int(np.ceil(float(args.surface_distance)/max(float(args.cell_size),1e-12))))
        nn=natural_neighbor_limited(tmp,maxpx); pred=nn[targets]
    elif base_method=='barnes':
        tmp=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,float); tmp[observed]=grid[observed]
        maxpx=max(1,int(np.ceil(float(args.surface_distance)/max(float(args.cell_size),1e-12))))
        bb=barnes_limited(tmp,maxpx,float(args.cell_size),max(3,int(args.idw_neighbors))); pred=bb[targets]
    elif base_method=='spline-tension':
        valid=np.isfinite(grid)&observed
        if not valid.any(): return out
        _,inds=distance_transform_edt(~valid,return_indices=True)
        work=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,float); work[observed]=grid[observed]; work[targets]=grid[inds[0][targets],inds[1][targets]]
        active=targets.copy(); tension=float(np.clip(args.spline_tension,0.0,1.0))
        for _ in range(max(20,int(args.spline_iterations))):
            old=work.copy(); q=np.pad(work,1,mode='edge')
            membrane=(q[:-2,1:-1]+q[2:,1:-1]+q[1:-1,:-2]+q[1:-1,2:])/4.0
            bending=(membrane*4.0+q[:-2,:-2]+q[:-2,2:]+q[2:,:-2]+q[2:,2:])/8.0
            candidate=tension*membrane+(1.0-tension)*bending
            work[active]=candidate[active]; work[observed]=grid[observed]
            delta=np.nanmax(np.abs(work[active]-old[active]))
            if np.isfinite(delta) and delta<float(args.spline_tolerance): break
        pred=work[targets]
    else:
        raise SystemExit('Unknown MagSurveyPy surface method %s. Recommended: survey-local, tin-linear, local-idw, linear, idw, ordinary-kriging-bounded. Experimental smooth methods remain available for comparison.'%method)
    rr,cc=np.nonzero(targets); good=np.isfinite(pred); out[rr[good],cc[good]]=pred[good]; out[observed]=grid[observed]
    # Apply guard. A *-bounded method forces at least local-envelope clamping.
    if base_method!='dipole-safe':
        if force_bounded:
            old=getattr(args,'interpolation_guard','dipole-safe'); args.interpolation_guard='bounded'
            try: out=archaeology_interpolation_guard(np.where(observed,grid,np.nan),out,args,base_method,args.cores)
            finally: args.interpolation_guard=old
        else:
            out=archaeology_interpolation_guard(np.where(observed,grid,np.nan),out,args,base_method,args.cores)
    return out

def _v12_write_tif(path, array, profile, method, source):
    import numpy as np
    import rasterio

    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)

    p=profile.copy()
    p.update(
        count=1,
        dtype="float32",
        compress="deflate",
        predictor=3,
        nodata=-9999.0,
    )

    with rasterio.open(path,"w",**p) as ds:
        a=np.asarray(array,float)
        ds.write(np.where(np.isfinite(a),a,-9999.0).astype("float32"),1)
        ds.update_tags(
            software="MagSurveyPy Archaeological Magnetometry Prospection Suite",
            software_version=APP_VERSION,
            author="Alexandru Hegyi, PhD",
            website="https://alexandruhegyi.com",
            product="supported continuous interpolation surface",
            interpolation_method=method,
            source=str(source),
            MULTIMAG_ARRAY_ORIENTATION="north-up",
            MULTIMAG_ROW0="maximum_northing",
        )


def _v12_surface_compare(argv):
    import argparse
    import csv
    import json
    import math
    import time
    import numpy as np
    import rasterio

    from pathlib import Path
    from scipy.ndimage import distance_transform_edt

    p=argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=(
            "MagSurveyPy full supported-surface interpolation comparison. "
            "Directly observed cells remain unchanged; interpolation predicts "
            "all supported gaps out to --surface-distance rather than only the "
            "short 0.5 m production fill distance."
        )
    )

    p.add_argument("input",type=Path,help="MagSurveyPy project folder or Bz GeoTIFF")
    p.add_argument("-o","--output",type=Path,required=True)
    p.add_argument("--source-product",default="Bz_grid")

    p.add_argument(
        "--methods",
        default="survey-local,tin-linear,local-idw,ordinary-kriging-bounded",
        help="Comma list: survey-local, tin-linear, tin-cubic, local-idw, nearest, idw, linear, bicubic-bounded, ordinary-kriging-bounded and optional experimental smooth methods. survey-local is the recommended magnetic-survey method: local TIN interpolation with positive-weight IDW fallback and measured-support bounds. tin-linear and local-idw use local non-overshooting mathematical interpolation suitable for dense archaeological magnetic data."
    )

    p.add_argument(
        "--surface-distance",
        type=float,
        default=1.0,
        help="Maximum distance from a directly observed cell for the supported surface. Default 1.0 m. For dense cart magnetometry, 0.5-1.0 m is usually safer than broad extrapolation."
    )

    p.add_argument("--cv-fraction",type=float,default=0.02)
    p.add_argument("--cv-max",type=int,default=10000)
    p.add_argument("--cv-seed",type=int,default=2026)

    p.add_argument("--idw-power",type=float,default=2.0)
    p.add_argument("--idw-neighbors",type=int,default=8)

    p.add_argument("--rbf-neighbors",type=int,default=32)
    p.add_argument("--rbf-smoothing",type=float,default=0.02)
    p.add_argument("--rbf-epsilon",type=float,default=1.0)
    p.add_argument("--kriging-neighbors",type=int,default=12,help="Local ordinary-kriging neighbours. Default 12.")
    p.add_argument("--kriging-range",type=float,default=2.0,metavar="METRES",help="Spherical variogram range. Default 2 m.")
    p.add_argument("--kriging-nugget",type=float,default=0.0,help="Ordinary-kriging nugget variance. Default 0.")
    p.add_argument("--interpolation-guard",choices=("off","bounded","dipole-safe"),default="dipole-safe",help="Default dipole-safe: bound every prediction to nearby measured support and replace high-order interpolation near strong magnetic anomalies with positive-weight local IDW. This is specifically intended to prevent white/black halos and ringing around dipolar responses. Use off only to inspect raw mathematical behavior.")
    p.add_argument("--dipole-sigma",type=float,default=3.0,metavar="SIGMA",help="Robust anomaly threshold for dipole protection. Default 3.0 sigma.")
    p.add_argument("--guard-radius",type=float,default=1.0,metavar="METRES",help="Protection radius around strong measured anomalies. Default 1.0 m.")
    p.add_argument("--guard-neighbors",type=int,default=12,help="Measured neighbours used for local bounds/safe prediction. Default 12.")
    p.add_argument("--overshoot-margin",type=float,default=0.03,metavar="FRACTION",help="Allowed local-range margin before clamping. Default 0.03.")
    p.add_argument("--guard-deviation",type=float,default=0.25,metavar="FRACTION",help="Strong-anomaly disagreement threshold between high-order and safe local interpolation. Default 0.25 of local range; smaller is more conservative.")

    p.add_argument("--spline-tension",type=float,default=0.65)
    p.add_argument("--spline-iterations",type=int,default=180)
    p.add_argument("--spline-tolerance",type=float,default=1e-4)

    p.add_argument("--max-surface-observations",type=int,default=120000)
    p.add_argument("--overshoot-sigma",type=float,default=5.0)
    p.add_argument("--cores",type=int,default=8)

    a=p.parse_args(argv)

    a.output.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)

    gd=a.output/"GeoTIFF"
    pd=a.output/"PNG"
    rd=a.output/"Reports"

    for d in (gd,pd,rd):
        d.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)

    if "_resolve_project_raster" in globals():
        source=_resolve_project_raster(a.input,a.source_product)
    else:
        source=a.input

    with rasterio.open(source) as ds:
        grid=ds.read(1).astype(float)
        nod=ds.nodata

        if nod is not None:
            grid[grid==nod]=np.nan

        profile=ds.profile.copy()
        cell=float(abs(ds.transform.a))
        a.cell_size=cell

    density_path=_v12_project_density_raster(source,a.input)

    if density_path:
        with rasterio.open(density_path) as ds:
            density=ds.read(1).astype(float)
            dn=ds.nodata

            if dn is not None:
                density[density==dn]=np.nan

        observed=np.isfinite(density)&(density>0)&np.isfinite(grid)

        print(f"Direct observations from: {density_path}",flush=True)
    else:
        observed=np.isfinite(grid)

        print(
            "WARNING: measurement-density raster was not found. "
            "All finite source cells are being treated as observed.",
            flush=True
        )

    if not observed.any():
        raise SystemExit("No directly observed cells found.")

    dist=distance_transform_edt(~observed)*cell

    domain=dist<=max(a.surface_distance,cell)
    targets=domain & ~observed

    methods=[]
    for m in a.methods.split(","):
        m=m.strip().lower()
        if m and m not in methods:
            methods.append(m)

    med=float(np.nanmedian(grid[observed]))
    sigma=_v12_robust_sigma(grid[observed])
    # Edge/gradient preservation reference. Missing cells are filled only for the
    # derivative calculation; directly observed magnetic values are never changed.
    _ref_for_grad=_nearest_fill_nan(np.where(observed,grid,np.nan))
    _rgy,_rgx=np.gradient(_ref_for_grad)
    _ref_grad=np.hypot(_rgx,_rgy)

    rng=np.random.default_rng(a.cv_seed)

    obs_idx=np.column_stack(np.nonzero(observed))

    ncv=min(
        a.cv_max,
        max(100,int(round(a.cv_fraction*len(obs_idx))))
    )

    cv_choice=rng.choice(len(obs_idx),size=min(ncv,len(obs_idx)),replace=False)

    cv_mask=np.zeros_like(observed,bool)
    cv_rc=obs_idx[cv_choice]
    cv_mask[cv_rc[:,0],cv_rc[:,1]]=True

    train_obs=observed & ~cv_mask

    rows=[]
    products={}

    print(
        f"MagSurveyPy surface comparison: observed={observed.sum():,}; "
        f"surface targets={targets.sum():,}; CV={cv_mask.sum():,}",
        flush=True
    )

    for method in methods:
        print(f"Surface interpolation: {method}",flush=True)
        _t0=time.perf_counter()

        try:
            surface=_v12_surface_predict(method,grid,observed,targets,a)
        except Exception as exc:
            print(f"Surface interpolation skipped ({method}): {exc}",file=sys.stderr,flush=True)
            continue

        products[method]=surface

        tif=gd/f"{Path(source).stem}_surface_{method}.tif"

        _v12_write_tif(
            tif,
            surface,
            profile,
            method,
            source
        )

        # Cross-validation.
        cv_grid=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,float)
        cv_grid[train_obs]=grid[train_obs]

        try:
            cv_pred=_v12_surface_predict(method,cv_grid,train_obs,cv_mask,a)
        except Exception as exc:
            print(f"Surface CV warning ({method}): {exc}",file=sys.stderr,flush=True)
            cv_pred=np.full_like(grid,np.nan,float)

        truth=grid[cv_mask]
        pred=cv_pred[cv_mask]

        good=np.isfinite(truth)&np.isfinite(pred)

        if good.any():
            err=pred[good]-truth[good]

            rmse=float(np.sqrt(np.mean(err*err)))
            mae=float(np.mean(np.abs(err)))
            bias=float(np.mean(err))
            ae=np.abs(err); medae=float(np.median(ae)); p90=float(np.percentile(ae,90)); p95=float(np.percentile(ae,95)); p99=float(np.percentile(ae,99)); maxerr=float(np.max(ae))
        else:
            rmse=mae=bias=medae=p90=p95=p99=maxerr=float("nan")

        # Strong-anomaly and bipolar-neighbourhood CV. This prevents a method
        # from winning merely because quiet background cells dominate global RMSE.
        strong=(np.abs(truth-med)>=max(float(a.dipole_sigma),0.5)*max(sigma,1e-9)) & good
        strong_rmse=float(np.sqrt(np.mean((pred[strong]-truth[strong])**2))) if np.any(strong) else float('nan')
        strong_mae=float(np.mean(np.abs(pred[strong]-truth[strong]))) if np.any(strong) else float('nan')
        strong_sign_error=float(np.mean(np.sign(pred[strong]-med)!=np.sign(truth[strong]-med))) if np.any(strong) else float('nan')
        # Local measured envelope and bipolar context from training neighbours.
        _trc=np.column_stack(np.nonzero(train_obs)).astype(float); _tvals=np.asarray(grid[train_obs],float)
        _crc=np.column_stack(np.nonzero(cv_mask)).astype(float)
        local_over=float('nan'); dipole_over=float('nan'); bipolar_rmse=float('nan'); bipolar_count=0
        if len(_trc) and len(_crc):
            _tree=cKDTree(_trc); _k=min(max(4,int(a.guard_neighbors)),len(_tvals)); _d,_ii=_tree.query(_crc,k=_k,workers=max(1,int(a.cores)))
            if _k==1: _ii=_ii[:,None]
            _nv=_tvals[_ii]; _lo=np.min(_nv,axis=1); _hi=np.max(_nv,axis=1); _span=np.maximum(_hi-_lo,1e-12); _margin=max(0.0,float(a.overshoot_margin))*_span
            _p=cv_pred[cv_mask]; _ok=np.isfinite(_p)
            _over=_ok & ((_p<_lo-_margin)|(_p>_hi+_margin)); local_over=float(np.mean(_over[_ok])) if np.any(_ok) else float('nan')
            _bip=(np.min(_nv,axis=1)<med-sigma) & (np.max(_nv,axis=1)>med+sigma)
            bipolar_count=int(np.sum(_bip & _ok))
            if bipolar_count:
                _bt=grid[cv_mask][_bip & _ok]; _bp=_p[_bip & _ok]
                bipolar_rmse=float(np.sqrt(np.mean((_bp-_bt)**2)))
                dipole_over=float(np.mean(_over[_bip & _ok]))

        sv=surface[np.isfinite(surface)]

        if len(sv):
            z=np.abs((sv-med)/max(sigma,1e-9))
            overs=float(np.mean(z>a.overshoot_sigma)); over3=float(np.mean(z>3.0)); over5=float(np.mean(z>5.0)); maxz=float(np.max(z))
        else:
            overs=over3=over5=maxz=float("nan")

        finite_surface=np.isfinite(surface)
        if finite_surface.any():
            _sf=_nearest_fill_nan(surface)
            _sgy,_sgx=np.gradient(_sf)
            _sgrad=np.hypot(_sgx,_sgy)
            _em=observed & np.isfinite(_ref_grad) & np.isfinite(_sgrad)
            if int(_em.sum())>10 and float(np.std(_ref_grad[_em]))>0 and float(np.std(_sgrad[_em]))>0:
                edge_corr=float(np.corrcoef(_ref_grad[_em],_sgrad[_em])[0,1])
            else:
                edge_corr=float('nan')
        else:
            edge_corr=float('nan')

        rows.append({
            "method":method,
            "cv_rmse":rmse,
            "cv_mae":mae,
            "cv_bias":bias,
            "cv_median_abs_error":medae, "cv_p90_abs_error":p90, "cv_p95_abs_error":p95, "cv_p99_abs_error":p99, "cv_max_abs_error":maxerr,
            "cv_prediction_fraction":float(good.mean()) if len(good) else 0.0,
            "cv_strong_anomaly_count":int(np.sum(strong)),
            "cv_strong_rmse":strong_rmse,
            "cv_strong_mae":strong_mae,
            "cv_strong_sign_error_fraction":strong_sign_error,
            "cv_bipolar_neighborhood_count":bipolar_count,
            "cv_bipolar_rmse":bipolar_rmse,
            "cv_local_envelope_overshoot_fraction":local_over,
            "cv_bipolar_envelope_overshoot_fraction":dipole_over,
            "surface_cells":int(finite_surface.sum()),
            "new_surface_cells":int((finite_surface&~observed).sum()),
            "new_surface_fraction_of_domain":float((finite_surface&~observed).sum()/max(domain.sum(),1)),
            "overshoot_gt_3sigma_fraction":over3, "overshoot_gt_5sigma_fraction":over5,
            "overshoot_gt_sigma_fraction":overs,
            "max_absolute_robust_z":maxz,
            "amplitude_retention":float(np.nanpercentile(np.abs(surface[np.isfinite(surface)]-np.nanmedian(surface)),95)/max(np.nanpercentile(np.abs(grid[observed]-np.nanmedian(grid[observed])),95),1e-12)) if finite_surface.any() else float('nan'),
            "edge_gradient_correlation":edge_corr,
            "roughness_rms_gradient":float(np.sqrt(np.nanmean(np.square(np.gradient(np.where(np.isfinite(surface),surface,np.nanmedian(surface)))[0]))+np.nanmean(np.square(np.gradient(np.where(np.isfinite(surface),surface,np.nanmedian(surface)))[1])))) if finite_surface.any() else float('nan'),
            "processing_time_s":float(time.perf_counter()-_t0), "memory_estimate_mb":float(surface.nbytes/1048576.0),
        })

        # Individual preview.
        try:
            import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

            f=surface[np.isfinite(surface)]

            if len(f):
                lim=float(np.percentile(np.abs(f-np.median(f)),98))
                lim=max(lim,1e-9)

                fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(10,8))
                im=ax.imshow(
                    surface,
                    origin="upper",
                    cmap="gray_r",
                    vmin=np.median(f)-lim,
                    vmax=np.median(f)+lim,
                )

                ax.set_title(f"{method} — supported surface")
                ax.set_xlabel("grid column")
                ax.set_ylabel("grid row")

                cb=fig.colorbar(im,ax=ax)
                cb.set_label("Magnetic value (nT/m)")

                fig.tight_layout()
                fig.savefig(
                    pd/f"{Path(source).stem}_surface_{method}.png",
                    dpi=220
                )

                plt.close(fig)

        except Exception as exc:
            print(f"Preview warning ({method}): {exc}",flush=True)

    # Score: global reconstruction + explicit strong/dipolar preservation.
    finite_rmse=[r['cv_rmse'] for r in rows if np.isfinite(r['cv_rmse'])]
    scale=max(np.median(finite_rmse),1e-9) if finite_rmse else 1.0
    finite_strong=[r['cv_strong_rmse'] for r in rows if np.isfinite(r.get('cv_strong_rmse',float('nan')))]
    strong_scale=max(np.median(finite_strong),scale,1e-9) if finite_strong else scale
    for r in rows:
        base=(r['cv_rmse']/scale) if np.isfinite(r['cv_rmse']) else 10.0
        sr=(r.get('cv_strong_rmse',float('nan'))/strong_scale) if np.isfinite(r.get('cv_strong_rmse',float('nan'))) else base
        br=(r.get('cv_bipolar_rmse',float('nan'))/strong_scale) if np.isfinite(r.get('cv_bipolar_rmse',float('nan'))) else sr
        local_over=r.get('cv_local_envelope_overshoot_fraction',float('nan')); local_over=local_over if np.isfinite(local_over) else 1.0
        dip_over=r.get('cv_bipolar_envelope_overshoot_fraction',float('nan')); dip_over=dip_over if np.isfinite(dip_over) else local_over
        signerr=r.get('cv_strong_sign_error_fraction',float('nan')); signerr=signerr if np.isfinite(signerr) else 0.0
        over=r.get('overshoot_gt_sigma_fraction',float('nan')); over=over if np.isfinite(over) else 1.0
        amp=abs(float(r.get('amplitude_retention',1.0))-1.0) if np.isfinite(r.get('amplitude_retention',float('nan'))) else 1.0
        bias=abs(r['cv_bias'])/scale if np.isfinite(r['cv_bias']) else 10.0
        r['selection_score']=float(base + 0.75*sr + 0.65*br + 5.0*local_over + 7.0*dip_over + 2.0*signerr + 2.0*over + 0.75*amp + 0.20*bias)

    ranked=sorted(rows,key=lambda r:r["selection_score"])

    # Production recommendation is intentionally restricted to interpolation
    # families whose predictions are convex/local and therefore cannot create
    # high-order ringing. Smooth/high-order methods can still win the
    # lowest-error comparison and remain fully reported, but are not silently
    # promoted over a non-overshooting magnetic-survey surface.
    _safe_methods={'survey-local','tin-linear','local-idw','linear','idw','nearest','dipole-safe','archaeology-safe'}
    _safe_ranked=[r for r in ranked if r.get('method') in _safe_methods]
    recommended=(_safe_ranked[0] if _safe_ranked else (ranked[0] if ranked else None))
    recommended=recommended['method'] if recommended else None

    # CSV.
    cp=rd/f"{Path(source).stem}_surface_interpolation_comparison.csv"

    with cp.open("w",newline="",encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fields=list(rows[0].keys()) if rows else ["method"]
        w=csv.DictWriter(fh,fieldnames=fields)
        w.writeheader()
        w.writerows(rows)

    # JSON.
    jp=rd/f"{Path(source).stem}_surface_interpolation_recommendation.json"

    summary={
        "software_version":APP_VERSION,
        "source":str(source),
        "density_source":str(density_path) if density_path else None,
        "cell_size_m":cell,
        "surface_distance_m":a.surface_distance,
        "artifact_guard":a.interpolation_guard,
        "dipole_sigma":a.dipole_sigma,
        "guard_radius_m":a.guard_radius,
        "guard_neighbors":a.guard_neighbors,
        "overshoot_margin":a.overshoot_margin,
        "guard_deviation":a.guard_deviation,
        "method_notes":{
            "survey-local":"local Delaunay/TIN with positive-weight IDW fallback and local measured-envelope bounds",
            "tin-linear":"Delaunay TIN piecewise-linear interpolation; non-overshooting inside each triangle",
            "tin-cubic":"Clough-Tocher TIN cubic candidate; artifact guard remains active",
            "local-idw":"positive inverse-distance weighting; prediction remains inside local sampled value range",
            "dipole-safe":"legacy alias of survey-local",
            "bicubic":"Clough-Tocher smooth piecewise-cubic analogue for scattered/missing support",
            "bicubic-bounded":"Clough-Tocher cubic plus local measured-envelope bound",
            "ordinary-kriging":"local ordinary kriging with spherical variogram",
            "ordinary-kriging-bounded":"ordinary kriging plus local measured-envelope bound"},
        "direct_observed_cells":int(observed.sum()),
        "surface_target_cells":int(targets.sum()),
        "recommended_method":recommended,
        "recommendation_policy":"Production recommendation is restricted to local/non-overshooting method families; smooth high-order methods remain reported separately as lowest-error candidates.",
        "lowest_error_method":min(rows,key=lambda r:r["cv_rmse"] if np.isfinite(r["cv_rmse"]) else float("inf"))["method"] if rows else None,
        "best_preservation_method":min(rows,key=lambda r:abs(r.get("amplitude_retention",1.0)-1.0)+r.get("overshoot_gt_5sigma_fraction",1.0)+0.5*max(0.0,1.0-(r.get("edge_gradient_correlation") if np.isfinite(r.get("edge_gradient_correlation",float('nan'))) else 0.0)))["method"] if rows else None,
        "recommended_balanced_method":recommended,
        "important_note":(
            "Observed magnetic cells are preserved exactly. "
            "Recommendation is based on held-out reconstruction plus an "
            "strong/bipolar-anomaly error and local-envelope/ringing penalties; it is not an archaeological classifier."
        ),
        "methods":ranked,
    }

    jp.write_text(json.dumps(summary,indent=2),encoding="utf-8")

    # Shared-range comparison PNG. This is intentionally a visual QC companion
    # to the numeric CV report, not the basis of method selection.
    try:
        import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
        show=ranked[:min(4,len(ranked))]
        if show:
            vals=grid[observed]
            center=float(np.nanmedian(vals)); lim=float(np.nanpercentile(np.abs(vals-center),98)); lim=max(lim,1e-9)
            n=len(show); fig,axs=plt.subplots(1,n,figsize=(4.2*n,4.4),squeeze=False)
            last=None
            for ax,r in zip(axs[0],show):
                m=r['method']; last=ax.imshow(products[m],origin='upper',cmap='gray_r',vmin=center-lim,vmax=center+lim)
                ax.set_title(f"{m}\nCV RMSE={r['cv_rmse']:.3g}; edge r={r.get('edge_gradient_correlation',float('nan')):.3f}")
                ax.set_xlabel('grid column'); ax.set_ylabel('grid row')
            if last is not None:
                cb=fig.colorbar(last,ax=list(axs[0]),orientation='horizontal',fraction=.055,pad=.16,aspect=45)
                cb.set_label('Magnetic value (nT/m) — shared scale')
            fig.suptitle('Supported-surface interpolation comparison — shared scale')
            fig.subplots_adjust(left=.06,right=.98,bottom=.22,top=.82,wspace=.30)
            fig.savefig(pd/f"{Path(source).stem}_surface_interpolation_comparison.png",dpi=220,bbox_inches='tight')
            plt.close(fig)
    except Exception as exc:
        print(f"Comparison preview warning: {exc}",flush=True)

    # HTML.
    hp=rd/f"{Path(source).stem}_surface_interpolation_report.html"

    trs=[]

    for r in ranked:
        trs.append(
            "<tr>"
            f"<td><b>{r['method']}</b></td>"
            f"<td>{r['cv_rmse']:.4f}</td>"
            f"<td>{r['cv_mae']:.4f}</td>"
            f"<td>{r['cv_bias']:+.4f}</td>"
            f"<td>{r['cv_p95_abs_error']:.4f}</td>"
            f"<td>{r.get('cv_strong_rmse',float('nan')):.4f}</td>"
            f"<td>{r.get('cv_bipolar_rmse',float('nan')):.4f}</td>"
            f"<td>{100*r.get('cv_local_envelope_overshoot_fraction',float('nan')):.2f}%</td>"
            f"<td>{r.get('edge_gradient_correlation',float('nan')):.3f}</td>"
            f"<td>{100*r['overshoot_gt_sigma_fraction']:.3f}%</td>"
            f"<td>{r['max_absolute_robust_z']:.2f}</td>"
            f"<td>{r['selection_score']:.3f}</td>"
            "</tr>"
        )

    hp.write_text(
        f"""<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>MagSurveyPy surface interpolation comparison</title>
<style>
body{{font-family:system-ui;max-width:1150px;margin:auto;padding:2rem;color:#172033}}
table{{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}}
td,th{{border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding:.55rem;text-align:right}}
td:first-child,th:first-child{{text-align:left}}
.rec{{padding:1rem;background:#ecfdf5;border-left:5px solid #059669}}
.note{{padding:1rem;background:#fff7ed;border-left:5px solid #ea580c}}
</style>
<h1>MagSurveyPy surface interpolation comparison</h1>

<div class="rec">
<b>Recommended method:</b> {recommended}
</div>

<p class="note">
This differs from gap-fill gap filling. Directly measured cells remain
unchanged, but interpolation is allowed over the complete supported
{a.surface_distance:g} m neighbourhood. Large overshoots are explicitly
penalised.
</p>

<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>CV RMSE</th>
<th>CV MAE</th>
<th>Bias</th>
<th>P95 error</th>
<th>Edge r</th>
<th>&gt;{a.overshoot_sigma:g}σ</th>
<th>Max |z|</th>
<th>Score</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
{''.join(trs)}
</tbody>
</table>
""",
        encoding="utf-8"
    )

    print("",flush=True)
    print("MagSurveyPy surface interpolation complete.",flush=True)
    print(f"Recommended: {recommended}",flush=True)
    print(f"Report: {hp}",flush=True)

    return 0


def _v12_adaptive_denoise(argv):
    import argparse
    import csv
    import json
    import shutil
    import subprocess
    import sys
    import numpy as np

    from pathlib import Path

    p=argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=(
            "MagSurveyPy adaptive archaeology denoising. "
            "Tests several conservative strengths rather than assuming that "
            "one strength is appropriate for every site."
        )
    )

    p.add_argument("input",type=Path)
    p.add_argument("-o","--output",type=Path,required=True)
    p.add_argument("--source-product",default="Bz_grid")

    p.add_argument(
        "--strengths",
        default="0.15,0.25,0.35,0.45,0.55"
    )

    p.add_argument("--cores",type=int,default=8)

    a=p.parse_args(argv)

    if "_resolve_project_raster" in globals():
        source=_resolve_project_raster(a.input,a.source_product)
    else:
        source=a.input

    strengths=[]

    for x in a.strengths.split(","):
        x=x.strip()

        if x:
            strengths.append(float(x))

    if not strengths:
        raise SystemExit("No adaptive strengths supplied.")

    root=a.output
    cand=root/"Candidates"
    rep=root/"Reports"
    geo=root/"GeoTIFF"

    for d in (cand,rep,geo):
        d.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)

    script=None

    all_rows=[]

    for strength in strengths:
        token=f"{strength:.2f}".replace(".","p")
        od=cand/f"strength_{token}"

        print(
            f"Adaptive denoise: testing strength {strength:.2f}",
            flush=True
        )

        cmd=[
            sys.executable,
            "-m",
            "magsurveypy.cli",
            "denoise",
            str(source),
            "-o",
            str(od),
            "--method",
            "archaeology",
            "--strength",
            str(strength),
            "--cores",
            str(a.cores),
        ]

        cp=subprocess.run(cmd)

        if cp.returncode!=0:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"Denoising candidate strength {strength} failed."
            )

        perf=list((od/"Reports").glob("*_filter_performance.csv"))

        if not perf:
            print(
                f"WARNING: no filter-performance report for {strength}",
                flush=True
            )
            continue

        with perf[0].open(newline="",encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for row in csv.DictReader(fh):
                if row.get("method")=="reference":
                    continue

                q=dict(row)
                q["strength"]=strength
                q["candidate_dir"]=str(od)

                for key in (
                    "balanced_score",
                    "preservation_score",
                    "noise_reduction_fraction",
                    "correlation_to_reference",
                    "edge_correlation",
                    "p95_anomaly_amplitude_ratio",
                    "fine_detail_ratio",
                    "rms_change_sigma",
                ):
                    try:
                        q[key]=float(q.get(key,"nan"))
                    except Exception:
                        q[key]=float("nan")

                q["safe_for_archaeology"]=str(
                    q.get("safe_for_archaeology","")
                ).lower() in {"true","1","yes"}

                all_rows.append(q)

    if not all_rows:
        raise SystemExit("No adaptive denoising candidates were produced.")

    safe=[r for r in all_rows if r["safe_for_archaeology"]]

    # Diagnostic weak-anomaly score: rewards noise reduction only when edge/fine
    # detail, correlation and amplitude remain close to the quantitative reference.
    # It is deliberately a ranking aid, not a claim that weak anomalies are real.
    def _finite(v,default=0.0):
        try:
            x=float(v); return x if np.isfinite(x) else default
        except Exception:
            return default
    for r in all_rows:
        nr=max(0.0,_finite(r.get("noise_reduction_fraction")))
        ed=max(0.0,min(1.2,_finite(r.get("edge_correlation"))))
        fd=max(0.0,min(1.2,_finite(r.get("fine_detail_ratio"))))
        co=max(0.0,min(1.2,_finite(r.get("correlation_to_reference"))))
        ar=_finite(r.get("p95_anomaly_amplitude_ratio"),1.0)
        rms=max(0.0,_finite(r.get("rms_change_sigma")))
        r["weak_anomaly_score"]=float(0.35*nr+0.25*ed+0.20*fd+0.20*co-0.35*abs(ar-1.0)-0.10*max(0.0,rms-0.5))

    balanced=max(
        safe or all_rows,
        key=lambda r:r["balanced_score"]
    )

    preservation=max(
        all_rows,
        key=lambda r:r["preservation_score"]
    )

    safe_denoise=max(
        safe or all_rows,
        key=lambda r:r["noise_reduction_fraction"]
    )

    weak_anomaly=max(
        safe or all_rows,
        key=lambda r:r.get("weak_anomaly_score",float("-inf"))
    )

    def label(r):
        return (
            f"{r['method']} @ strength "
            f"{float(r['strength']):.2f}"
        )

    summary={
        "software_version":APP_VERSION,
        "source":str(source),
        "tested_strengths":strengths,
        "candidate_count":len(all_rows),
        "safe_candidate_count":len(safe),
        "best_balanced":label(balanced),
        "best_preservation":label(preservation),
        "strongest_safe_denoising":label(safe_denoise),
        "best_weak_anomaly_enhancement":label(weak_anomaly),
        "weak_anomaly_note":"Diagnostic ranking only; archaeological reality still requires spatial/contextual interpretation and inspection of the removed component.",
        "policy":(
            "The original quantitative Bz remains the reference. "
            "A candidate is not accepted merely because it is smoother."
        ),
    }

    # Combined CSV.
    fields=[
        "method",
        "strength",
        "safe_for_archaeology",
        "balanced_score",
        "preservation_score",
        "noise_reduction_fraction",
        "correlation_to_reference",
        "edge_correlation",
        "p95_anomaly_amplitude_ratio",
        "fine_detail_ratio",
        "rms_change_sigma",
        "weak_anomaly_score",
        "candidate_dir",
    ]

    cp=rep/"adaptive_denoise_candidates.csv"

    with cp.open("w",newline="",encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        w=csv.DictWriter(fh,fieldnames=fields)
        w.writeheader()

        for r in sorted(
            all_rows,
            key=lambda x:x["balanced_score"],
            reverse=True
        ):
            w.writerow({k:r.get(k,"") for k in fields})

    jp=rep/"adaptive_denoise_recommendation.json"

    jp.write_text(
        json.dumps(
            {
                **summary,
                "best_balanced_metrics":balanced,
                "best_preservation_metrics":preservation,
                "strongest_safe_metrics":safe_denoise,
                "best_weak_anomaly_metrics":weak_anomaly,
            },
            indent=2,
            default=str,
        ),
        encoding="utf-8"
    )

    # Copy best-balanced raster if identifiable.
    method=str(balanced["method"])
    cdir=Path(balanced["candidate_dir"])

    options=list(
        (cdir/"GeoTIFF").glob(f"*_restored_{method}.tif")
    )

    if not options:
        options=list(
            (cdir/"GeoTIFF").glob(f"*_{method}.tif")
        )

    copied=None

    if options:
        copied=geo/"adaptive_best_balanced.tif"
        shutil.copy2(options[0],copied)

    hp=rep/"adaptive_denoise_report.html"

    hp.write_text(
        f"""<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>MagSurveyPy adaptive denoising</title>
<style>
body{{font-family:system-ui;max-width:1050px;margin:auto;padding:2rem;color:#172033}}
.box{{padding:1rem;background:#ecfdf5;border-left:5px solid #059669;margin:.8rem 0}}
.note{{padding:1rem;background:#fff7ed;border-left:5px solid #ea580c}}
</style>

<h1>MagSurveyPy adaptive archaeological denoising</h1>

<div class="box">
<b>Best balanced:</b> {summary['best_balanced']}<br>
<b>Best preservation:</b> {summary['best_preservation']}<br>
<b>Strongest safe denoising:</b> {summary['strongest_safe_denoising']}<br>
<b>Best weak-anomaly diagnostic:</b> {summary['best_weak_anomaly_enhancement']}
</div>

<p class="note">
The original Bz raster remains the quantitative reference. MagSurveyPy tests
multiple strengths automatically and separates preservation from maximum noise
reduction.
</p>

<p>
Candidates tested: {len(all_rows)}<br>
Candidates passing the archaeology preservation gate: {len(safe)}
</p>

<p>
Full candidate metrics: <code>{cp.name}</code>
</p>
""",
        encoding="utf-8"
    )

    print("",flush=True)
    print("Adaptive denoising complete.",flush=True)
    print(f"Best balanced: {summary['best_balanced']}",flush=True)
    print(f"Best preservation: {summary['best_preservation']}",flush=True)
    print(
        f"Strongest safe denoising: "
        f"{summary['strongest_safe_denoising']}",
        flush=True
    )
    print(f"Best weak-anomaly diagnostic: {summary['best_weak_anomaly_enhancement']}",flush=True)

    if copied:
        print(f"Best-balanced raster: {copied}",flush=True)

    print(f"Report: {hp}",flush=True)

    return 0



def _safe_project_name(value):
    if value is None: return None
    x=str(value).strip().replace('\\','/')
    if not x or x in {'.','..'} or '/' in x:
        raise SystemExit('--project must be a single project/site name, not a path')
    x=re.sub(r'[^A-Za-z0-9._-]+','_',x).strip('._-')
    if not x: raise SystemExit('Invalid --project name')
    return x


def _infer_project_from_input(argv, results_root):
    if not argv: return None
    # Commands in this application put their main input first. Ignore action-only
    # AI corpus commands and options.
    first=None
    for x in argv:
        sx=str(x)
        if sx.startswith('-'): continue
        first=Path(sx); break
    if first is None: return None
    parts=first.parts; rr=Path(results_root)
    if not rr.is_absolute() and rr.parts and len(parts)>=2 and parts[0]==rr.parts[0]:
        return _safe_project_name(parts[1])
    # Legacy Results/Rupea_NATIVE style: infer only recognised stage suffixes.
    if not rr.is_absolute() and len(parts)>=2 and parts[0]==rr.parts[0] and '_' in parts[1]:
        head,tail=parts[1].split('_',1)
        if tail.upper().startswith(('NATIVE','SURFACE','CLEAN','REGULAR','ENHANCED','SEGMENT','THIN','BACKGROUND','PLOUGH','DENOISE')):
            return _safe_project_name(head)
    return None


def _extract_global_routing(argv):
    """Remove global routing options and return (argv, results_root, project)."""
    vals=list(argv); root=Path('Results'); project=None; clean=[]; i=0
    while i<len(vals):
        x=str(vals[i])
        if x=='--results-root':
            if i+1>=len(vals): raise SystemExit('--results-root requires a directory')
            root=Path(vals[i+1]); i+=2; continue
        if x.startswith('--results-root='):
            root=Path(x.split('=',1)[1]); i+=1; continue
        if x=='--project':
            if i+1>=len(vals): raise SystemExit('--project requires a project name')
            project=_safe_project_name(vals[i+1]); i+=2; continue
        if x.startswith('--project='):
            project=_safe_project_name(x.split('=',1)[1]); i+=1; continue
        clean.append(vals[i]); i+=1
    if project is None:
        # Explicit --site-name is the best automatic project identity.
        for j,x in enumerate(clean):
            sx=str(x)
            if sx=='--site-name' and j+1<len(clean): project=_safe_project_name(clean[j+1]); break
            if sx.startswith('--site-name='): project=_safe_project_name(sx.split('=',1)[1]); break
    if project is None:
        project=_infer_project_from_input(clean,root)
    return clean,root,project


def _stage_name_for_project(output_name, project, command):
    name=str(output_name)
    if project and name.lower().startswith(project.lower()+'_'):
        name=name[len(project)+1:]
    if project and name.lower()==project.lower():
        name=command.upper()
    return name or command.upper()


def _route_output_argv(command, argv, results_root=Path('Results'), project=None):
    """Route simple outputs to Results/PROJECT/STAGE; explicit paths are preserved."""
    vals=list(argv); idx=None
    for i,x in enumerate(vals):
        if str(x) in {'-o','--output'} and i+1<len(vals): idx=i+1; break
        if str(x).startswith('--output='):
            p=Path(str(x).split('=',1)[1])
            if (not p.is_absolute()) and len(p.parts)==1 and p.name not in {'.','..'}:
                stage=_stage_name_for_project(p.name,project,command)
                p=(Path(results_root)/project/stage) if project else (Path(results_root)/stage)
                vals[i]='--output='+str(p)
            print(f'PROJECT: {project}' if project else 'PROJECT: (unassigned)',flush=True)
            print(f'RESOLVED OUTPUT: {p}',flush=True); return vals
    defaults={'prm':'MULTICHANNEL','process':'GRID','grid':'GRID','point-grid':'POINT_GRID','enhance':'ENHANCED','segment':'SEGMENT','surface-compare':'SURFACE','clean-map':'CLEAN','regularize-points':'REGULAR025','background':'BACKGROUND','plough':'PLOUGH','denoise':'DENOISE','denoise-adaptive':'DENOISE_ADAPTIVE','thin':'THIN','overlap-clean':'OVERLAP','presentation':'PRESENTATION','filter':'FILTER','total-field':'TOTAL_FIELD'}
    if idx is None and command in defaults:
        stage=defaults[command]; p=(Path(results_root)/project/stage) if project else (Path(results_root)/stage)
        vals += ['-o',str(p)]; print(f'PROJECT: {project}' if project else 'PROJECT: (unassigned)',flush=True); print(f'RESOLVED OUTPUT: {p}',flush=True); return vals
    if idx is not None:
        p=Path(vals[idx])
        if (not p.is_absolute()) and len(p.parts)==1 and p.name not in {'.','..'}:
            stage=_stage_name_for_project(p.name,project,command)
            p=(Path(results_root)/project/stage) if project else (Path(results_root)/stage)
            vals[idx]=str(p)
        print(f'PROJECT: {project}' if project else 'PROJECT: (unassigned)',flush=True)
        print(f'RESOLVED OUTPUT: {p}',flush=True)
    return vals

def _run_command(command, argv):
    mapping={'prm':prm_main,'process':process_main,'assemble':assemble_main,'grid':grid_main,'enhance':enhance_main,'segment':segment_main,'image':image_main,'gnss':gnss_main}
    fn=mapping[command]
    old=sys.argv[:]
    try:
        sys.argv=[f'{Path(old[0]).name} {command}',*list(argv)]
        return fn()
    finally:
        sys.argv=old



